//? Title: Finite-width ReLU neural tangent kernels
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Compute the parameter-gradient Gram matrix for a two-layer network at
initialization. Compare the kernels at widths 32, 512, and 8,192 with
the infinite-width expression and fit a ridge predictor with each kernel.
)

> From a parameter Jacobian to a kernel

A kernel entry is the dot product between the parameter gradients of the
network output on two inputs. Here the scalar output is the sum of
weighted ReLU activations divided by the square root of hidden width.
The gradient with respect to the output weights contributes an activation
Gram matrix. Differentiating through the hidden weights contributes a
second term that combines input dot products, ReLU gates, and squared
output weights.

Under independent standard normal weights, the infinite-width limit
depends on the input norms and the angle between them. The probability
that both Gaussian projections are positive is \((\pi-\theta)/(2\pi)\).
The limiting kernel includes the contributions from differentiating
both layers. The finite implementation computes the same Gram matrix
through matrix products without forming the full parameter Jacobian.

> Compare the three widths on the upstream fixture

The first 18 rows fit the kernel ridge model and the remaining ten rows
measure its predictions. Each width draws its own initial weights.
The report checks its observed convergence against the limiting kernel
and compares predictions with a ridge model fitted using that limit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/neural_tangent_kernel.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    namespace nn = dm::nn;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    constexpr int rows = 28, fit = 18, features = 4;
    nn::set_num_threads(2);
    std::mt19937 rng(83145);
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal;
    const auto draw = [&](int n) {
        std::vector<double> out(n);
        for (auto& v : out) v = normal(rng);
        return out;
    };
    const auto vectors = draw(rows * features);
    std::vector<double> labels(rows);
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
        const int j = i * features;
        labels[i] = .5 * vectors[j] - .2 * vectors[j + 1] + .1 * vectors[j] * vectors[j + 2];
    }
    const auto x = nn::from_vector(vectors, {rows, features});
    const auto y = nn::from_vector(labels, {rows, 1});
    const auto actual = nn::slice_axis(y, 0, fit, rows);
    const auto prediction = [&](const auto& gram) {
        const auto fitting_columns = nn::slice_axis(gram, 1, 0, fit);
        const auto system = nn::slice_axis(fitting_columns, 0, 0, fit) + .025 * nn::eye(fit, nn::DType::Float64);
        const auto alpha = nn::solve(system, nn::slice_axis(y, 0, 0, fit));
        return nn::matmul(nn::slice_axis(fitting_columns, 0, fit, rows), alpha);
    };
    const auto limit = nn::relu_neural_tangent_kernel_limit(x);
    const auto reference = prediction(limit);
    nn::NamedTensors archive{{"x", x}, {"target", y}, {"limit", limit}, {"limit_prediction", reference}};
    std::vector<double> log_widths, errors, indices(rows - fit);
    std::iota(indices.begin(), indices.end(), 1);
    auto compare = g::RPlot::create().title("Kernel ridge predictions")
        .x_label("Held-out row").y_label("Response")
        .points(indices, actual.to_vector<double>(), "Target", {45, 48, 55})
        .lines(indices, reference.to_vector<double>(), "Infinite width", {35, 115, 175});
    for (int width : {32, 512, 8192}) {
        const auto w = nn::from_vector(draw(features * width), {features, width});
        const auto a = nn::from_vector(draw(width));
        const auto gram = nn::relu_neural_tangent_kernel(x, w, a);
        const auto pred = prediction(gram);
        const double error = std::sqrt((nn::sum(nn::square(gram - limit)) / nn::sum(nn::square(limit))).item());
        if (!errors.empty() && !(error < errors.back()))
            throw std::runtime_error("Kernel error does not decrease on the upstream fixture");
        errors.push_back(error);
        log_widths.push_back(std::log2(width));
        const auto suffix = std::to_string(width);
        archive.emplace_back("kernel_" + suffix, gram);
        archive.emplace_back("prediction_" + suffix, pred);
        if (width == 8192)
            compare.lines(indices, pred.to_vector<double>(), "Width 8192", {189, 67, 45});
        std::print("Width {}. Relative kernel error {:.6f}. Prediction MSE versus limit {:.6g}.\n\n",
                   width, error, nn::mean(nn::square(pred - reference)).item());
    }
    if (errors.back() > .08) throw std::runtime_error("Wide kernel differs from its limit");
    auto convergence = g::RPlot::create().title("Convergence on the fixed fixture")
        .x_label("Hidden width").y_label("Relative Frobenius error")
        .x_ticks(log_widths, {"32", "512", "8192"})
        .lines(log_widths, errors, "Empirical kernel", {189, 67, 45});
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    g::RLayout::create(1, 2).size(1000, 410).add(convergence).add(compare)
        .save_svg("build/neural_tangent_kernel.svg");
    nn::save_npz("build/neural_tangent_kernel.npz", archive);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Width 32. Relative kernel error 0.495540. Prediction MSE versus limit 0.00720527.

Width 512. Relative kernel error 0.071620. Prediction MSE versus limit 6.39159e-05.

Width 8192. Relative kernel error 0.016437. Prediction MSE versus limit 4.53718e-05.

\image(figures/neural_tangent_kernel.svg)
// result_end

The left plot shows relative kernel error on the fixed 28-row matrix.
The right plot compares the wide and limiting readouts on the held-out
rows. The decreasing error is a result of this fixture. It is not a
guarantee that independent random draws will improve monotonically.

> Reference

- [Jacot, Gabriel and Hongler, Neural Tangent Kernel|https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.07572].
